section: networking
title: Networking
about: |
  The zones, the subnets, the CIDRs, the routes, and the endpoints that a build needs. The section
  with the most rows and the least evidence: the lab runs go here first.
rules:
- id: eks-one-zone
  title: EKS needs subnets in two zones
  engines:
  - eks
  tier: static
  severity: fail
  status: source
  what: |
    EKS needs subnets in two availability zones or more. Palette checks the count of the control
    plane subnets, not the count of distinct zones. So a cluster with one zone passes each check
    of Palette and fails during the build.
  check:
    kind: static
    expr: |
      def zones: [ (.cloudConfig.azs // [])[], (.cloudConfig.subnets // [])[].az ] | map(select(. != null and . != "")) | unique;
      [ .entity.spec.machinepoolconfig[]
        | select((.cloudConfig.azs // []) != [] or (.cloudConfig.subnets // []) != [])
        | select((zones | length) < 2)
        | "pool \(.poolConfig.name) names \(zones | length | count_of("zone")) (\(zones | join(", "))); EKS needs 2 or more" ]
  fix:
    owner: palette
    text: Check the count of distinct zones in the EKS validate route.
  reproduce:
  - aws ec2 describe-subnets --region {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.region }} --filters Name=vpc-id,Values={{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.vpcId }} --query 'Subnets[].AvailabilityZone' --output text | tr '\t' '\n' | sort -u
  evidence:
  - kind: report
    ref: palette-precreate-validation-gaps-findings-2026-09-28 F6
  - kind: source
    note: no minimum count of zones for EKS
  tests:
  - fault: fixtures/eks/one-zone.json
    clean: fixtures/eks/clean.json
- id: eks-unsupported-zone
  title: Each zone of the pools takes an EKS cluster
  engines:
  - eks
  tier: cloud
  severity: fail
  status: inferred
  what: |
    EKS takes no cluster subnet in the zones use1-az3 (us-east-1), usw1-az2 (us-west-1), and
    cac1-az3 (ca-central-1), and none in a Local Zone or a Wavelength Zone. The build fails with
    `UnsupportedAvailabilityZoneException`. The zone id behind a name such as us-east-1e differs
    for each account, so the check asks EC2 for the ids of the zones the pools name. Palette does
    not check the zone.

    The zone route of Palette (`GET /v1/clouds/aws/regions/{region}/availabilityzones`) lists each
    zone of the account with `name`, `state`, and `zoneId`. It lists no zone type, and the test for a
    Local Zone or a Wavelength Zone reads the zone type. So the check stays at the cloud tier.
  when: ([ .entity.spec.machinepoolconfig[]? | (.cloudConfig.azs // [])[], ((.cloudConfig.subnets // [])[] | .az // empty) ] | map(select(. != null and . != "")) | unique | length) > 0
  check:
    kind: exec
    needs:
    - aws
    argv:
    - aws
    - ec2
    - describe-availability-zones
    - --region
    - '{{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.region }}'
    - --filters
    - Name=zone-name,Values={{ [ .entity.spec.machinepoolconfig[]? | (.cloudConfig.azs // [])[], ((.cloudConfig.subnets // [])[] | .az // empty) ] | map(select(. != null and . != "")) | unique | join(",") }}
    - --output
    - json
    expr: |
      def zones: [ .entity.spec.machinepoolconfig[]? | (.cloudConfig.azs // [])[], ((.cloudConfig.subnets // [])[] | .az // empty) ] | map(select(. != null and . != "")) | unique;
      .entity.spec.cloudConfig.region as $region | zones as $want
      | if .exec.exit != 0 then ["aws ec2 describe-availability-zones failed: \(.exec.stderr | .[:200])"]
        else (.exec.stdout.AvailabilityZones // []) as $z
          | ["use1-az3", "usw1-az2", "cac1-az3"] as $refused
          | [ ($want[] | select(. as $n | ($z | map(.ZoneName) | index($n)) == null) | "zone \(.) is not in region \($region) for this account"),
              ($z[] | . as $zone | select(($refused | index($zone.ZoneId)) != null) | "zone \(.ZoneName) is \(.ZoneId): EKS takes no cluster subnet in it"),
              ($z[] | select((.ZoneType // "availability-zone") != "availability-zone") | "zone \(.ZoneName) is a \(.ZoneType): EKS takes no cluster subnet in a Local Zone or a Wavelength Zone"),
              ($z[] | select((.State // "available") != "available") | "zone \(.ZoneName) is \(.State), not available") ]
        end
  reproduce:
  - aws ec2 describe-availability-zones --region {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.region }} --query 'AvailabilityZones[].[ZoneName,ZoneId,ZoneType,State]' --output table
  fix:
    owner: palette
    text: Check the zone ids of the pools against the list of EKS in the validate route.
  evidence:
  - kind: doc
    ref: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/network-reqs.html
    note: the table of disallowed zone ids, and no Outposts or Wavelength subnet for the cluster
  tests:
  - fault: fixtures/eks/zone-use1-az3.json
    clean: fixtures/eks/static-vpc-clean.json
- id: aws-dynamic-cidr-overlap
  title: The pod and service CIDRs stay outside the VPC of dynamic placement
  engines:
  - aws
  tier: static
  severity: fail
  status: source
  what: |
    For dynamic placement (no vpcId) Palette makes the VPC with 10.0.0.0/16. A pod CIDR or a
    service CIDR of the Kubernetes pack inside it breaks the routes of the nodes. Palette checks
    the CIDRs against the VPC for static placement alone, and skips the check when vpcId is
    empty. The check reads `pack.podCIDR` and `pack.serviceClusterIpRange`, or the
    kubeadm `networking.podSubnet` and `serviceSubnet`, of the Kubernetes pack.
  when: (.profiles | length) > 0
  check:
    kind: static
    expr: |
      def is_cidr: type == "string" and test("^([0-9]{1,3}\\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}/[0-9]{1,2}$");
      def ip($s): $s | split(".") | map(tonumber) | reduce .[] as $o (0; . * 256 + $o);
      def net($cidr; $p): ip($cidr | split("/")[0]) as $n | reduce range(0; 32 - $p) as $i ($n; (. / 2) | floor);
      def overlaps($a; $b): ([($a | split("/")[1] | tonumber), ($b | split("/")[1] | tonumber)] | min) as $p | net($a; $p) == net($b; $p);
      def k8s_cidrs: [ .profiles[]? | .packs[]? | select(.layer == "k8s") | .name as $n | .valuesCanon as $v
        | {name: $n, field: "pack.podCIDR", cidr: ($v.pack.podCIDR // $v.kubeadmconfig.clusterConfiguration.networking.podSubnet)},
          {name: $n, field: "pack.serviceClusterIpRange", cidr: ($v.pack.serviceClusterIpRange // $v.kubeadmconfig.clusterConfiguration.networking.serviceSubnet)}
        | select(.cidr | is_cidr) ];
      if (.entity.spec.cloudConfig.vpcId // "") != "" then []
      else [ k8s_cidrs[] | select(overlaps(.cidr; "10.0.0.0/16"))
             | "pack \(.name) sets \(.field) \(.cidr), which overlaps the VPC CIDR 10.0.0.0/16 of dynamic placement; Palette skips this check when vpcId is empty" ] end
  fix:
    owner: palette
    text: Run the CIDR check against 10.0.0.0/16 for dynamic placement too.
  evidence:
  - kind: source
    note: Palette skips the CIDR check when vpcId is empty; for static placement it tests the VPC CIDR against pack.podCIDR and pack.serviceClusterIpRange
  - kind: source
    note: 10.0.0.0/16, which Palette uses for the VPC of dynamic placement
  tests:
  - fault: fixtures/aws/pod-cidr-in-vpc.json
    clean: fixtures/aws/clean.json
